<a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
</div>
<h2 id="injury">What Happened</h2>
<p>Jefferson was hurt late in the first quarter at Tampa Bay, at the end of a 10-yard screen catch. He never needed help off the field, but after an extended visit to the medical tent he was ruled out early in the second half, finishing with two catches for 32 yards in a 23-16 Vikings win. The injury was diagnosed as a sprained left ankle, and head coach Kevin O'Connell described it as relatively mild. Jefferson did not practice during Week 4 prep and was ruled out against Miami, though O'Connell did not rule out a return for the Week 5 game at New Orleans.</p>
<div class="stat-card">
<div class="stat-icon">🩹</div>
<div class="stat-title">Justin Jefferson — Injury Snapshot</div>
<div class="stat-sub">Week 3 at Tampa Bay Buccaneers · Left Ankle Sprain</div>
<div class="stat-numbers">
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">13</div><div class="label">Receptions, Weeks 1–3</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">179</div><div class="label">Receiving Yards, Weeks 1–3</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">18</div><div class="label">Snaps With Murray This Season</div></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>The timing is especially frustrating. After a down 2025 in which Jefferson finished with 1,048 yards and a career-low 12.5 yards per catch, the Vikings replaced J.J. McCarthy with Kyler Murray, hoping to restore the offense that produced 1,533 yards and 10 touchdowns from Jefferson in 2024. Murray suffered a concussion in Week 1, and Jefferson has now been hurt in the game Murray returned. By reporting, the two have shared the field for only 18 snaps all season, and Jefferson's 179 yards are the fewest he has had through three weeks of a season.</p>
<h2 id="combine">The 2020 Combine Profile</h2>
<p>Jefferson was not expected to test like an elite athlete. On tape he looked like a smooth, precise route runner rather than a burner, and many analysts wanted to see a fast forty to quiet concerns about his top-end speed. He delivered: an official 4.43-second 40 at 202 pounds, tied for ninth among receivers in his class and faster than Jerry Jeudy, CeeDee Lamb and Jalen Reagor, all of whom were first-round options. He added a 37.5-inch vertical and a 126-inch (10'6") broad jump, both 13th among participants.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Measurable</th><th>Justin Jefferson (2020)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Height / Weight</td><td>6'1", 202 lbs</td></tr>
<tr><td>40-Yard Dash</td><td>4.43s (tied 9th among WRs)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vertical Jump</td><td>37.5"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Broad Jump</td><td>10'6" (126")</td></tr>
<tr><td>Draft Slot</td><td>No. 22 overall, Minnesota Vikings</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The numbers are good rather than record-setting, and that is the point: Jefferson's game has never depended on one outlier trait. A 4.43 forty at 202 pounds, paired with a 37.5-inch vertical, gives him enough speed to threaten vertically and enough explosiveness to win contested catches, while his route running does the rest. Jefferson skipped the shuttle and 3-cone, so there is no official agility test, which makes his on-field separation a stronger argument for his quickness than any drill.</p>
